.TH TRASH 1 "November 23, 2026" "trash 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
trash \- safe rm replacement with recovery
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B trash
[\fIOPTIONS\fR] \fIPATH\fR...
.br
.B trash
\fICOMMAND\fR [\fIOPTIONS\fR] [\fIARGS\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B trash
moves files and directories into a private trash store and can restore or
permanently delete them later. It never overwrites an existing file on restore;
conflicts receive a \fB~N\fR suffix.
.PP
The first non-flag argument is always a command: to trash a file named
\fBlist\fR use \fBtrash ./list\fR.
.PP
\fBlist\fR, \fBrestore\fR, and \fBempty\fR share one selection model: the
current directory by default, its subtree with \fB-r\fR, or the whole trash
with \fB--all\fR. Targets, filters, sorting, limits, and interactive selection
apply to all three commands in the same way.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B put
Move files and directories to the trash. This is the default when no command is
given.
.TP
.B list
List items trashed from the current directory. Use \fB-r\fR for the subtree and
\fB--all\fR for the whole trash.
.TP
.B restore
Restore the selected items. With no arguments, restore everything trashed from
the current directory. Paths match every version of that path.
.TP
.B empty
Permanently delete the selected items. With no arguments, delete items trashed
from the current directory. Use \fB--all\fR for the whole trash. Broken items
and orphan payloads are only deleted with \fB--include\-broken\fR. This cannot
be undone.
.TP
.B where
Print the payload path of each selected item inside the trash, one per line.
Refs are resolved from the arguments or, when stdin is not a terminal, from one
ref per line on stdin. Multiple refs produce one path per match, in argument
order, with duplicates dropped; \fBwhere\fR prints no metadata, so use
\fBlist\fR for that. Unresolvable refs read from stdin are reported as
warnings and skipped, and the exit status is non-zero; an unresolvable explicit
target is an error and nothing is printed.
.TP
.B stats
Show the trash location, the item count, and the total size of the items whose
size is already known. Sizes are measured lazily; see \fBSIZES\fR.
.TP
.B doctor
Report broken trash items and maintain the SQLite index. With \fB--repair\fR,
finish interrupted put, restore and empty operations (using the payload as
evidence) and measure the size of every item whose size is still unknown; with
\fB--doctor-purge\fR, remove broken items. \fB--integrity-check\fR runs
SQLite's integrity check, \fB--compact\fR vacuums the index, \fB--reindex\fR
rebuilds its b-trees, \fB--backup\fR writes \fBindex.backup.db\fR, and
\fB--rebuild\fR recreates rows from payloads (original paths are lost, so
rebuilt items cannot be restored in place).
.TP
.B help
Show help for a command.
.TP
.B version
Show the program version.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-store \fIPATH\fR
Use \fIPATH\fR as the trash store. Defaults to \fB$TRASH_STORE\fR,
\fB$XDG_DATA_HOME/trash\fR, or \fB~/.local/share/trash\fR.
.TP
.B \-r, \-\-recursive
Required to trash a directory. With \fBlist\fR, \fBrestore\fR,
\fBempty\fR, or \fBwhere\fR, include the current directory subtree, or the
subtree of a directory target.
.TP
.B \-\-preview
With \fBput\fR, show a preview and ask for confirmation before trashing.
.TP
.B \-i, \-\-interactive
With \fBput\fR, confirm each file before trashing. With \fBrestore\fR or
\fBempty\fR, choose items interactively with multi-select syntax such as
\fB1,3-5\fR, \fBa\fR for all, or \fBq\fR to quit.
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
Show what would happen without changing anything.
.TP
.B \-y, \-\-yes
Do not ask for confirmation. Required for non-interactive multi-item
\fBrestore\fR and \fBempty\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-all
Use the whole trash instead of the current directory. Cannot be combined with
targets or \fB-r\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-ids
With \fBlist\fR, print only full item ids, one per line, without the table
header or footer. Cannot be combined with \fB--json\fR or \fB--long\fR. Use it
to pipe a filtered selection into \fBwhere\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Print machine-readable JSON where supported.
.TP
.B \-\-long, \-\-full\-id
Show full ids, kinds, and full timestamps in \fBlist\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-limit \fIN\fR
Use at most \fIN\fR items after sorting.
.TP
.B \-\-sort \fIMODE\fR
Sort by \fBdate\fR (newest first, the default), \fBsize\fR, \fBpath\fR, or
\fBkind\fR. Sorting by \fBsize\fR measures every candidate first.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-size
With \fBlist\fR, do not measure unknown item sizes. Such items show \fB-\fR in
the size column and are left out of the total. Cannot be combined with
\fB--sort size\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-kind \fIKIND\fR
Filter by \fBfile\fR, \fBdir\fR, \fBsymlink\fR, or \fBother\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-grep \fIPATTERN\fR
Filter by a substring of the original path.
.TP
.B \-\-older\-than \fIAGE\fR
Filter items older than an age such as \fB30d\fR, \fB12h\fR, \fB5m\fR, or
\fB10s\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-keep \fIN\fR
With \fBempty\fR, never delete the \fIN\fR newest items of the selection,
even when filters match them.
.TP
.B \-\-repair
With \fBdoctor\fR, finish interrupted operations and measure sizes.
.TP
.B \-\-integrity\-check
With \fBdoctor\fR, run SQLite's \fBintegrity_check\fR and print the result.
.TP
.B \-\-compact
With \fBdoctor\fR, vacuum the index and release free pages.
.TP
.B \-\-backup
With \fBdoctor\fR, write a consistent snapshot to \fBindex.backup.db\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-rebuild
With \fBdoctor\fR, recreate rows from payloads. Original paths are not stored
elsewhere, so rebuilt items cannot be restored in place.
.TP
.B \-\-reindex
With \fBdoctor\fR, rebuild the index b-trees.
.TP
.B \-\-doctor\-purge
With \fBdoctor\fR, permanently remove broken items.
.TP
.B \-\-wait
With \fBempty\fR, wait for permanent deletion instead of deferring GC.
.TP
.B \-\-include\-broken
With \fBempty \-\-all\fR, also permanently delete broken items and orphan
payloads (item directories without an index row). Without this flag such
payloads are reported but never deleted, matching \fBdoctor\fR's default.
.TP
.B \-\-verbose
Explain what is happening.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-color
Disable colours. The \fBNO_COLOR\fR environment variable is also honoured.
.TP
.B \-\-reset\-store
Wipe the store when its index schema is incompatible instead of asking for
confirmation. Permanently deletes every trashed item.
.TP
.B \-h, \-\-help
Show this help. With a command, show help for that command.
.TP
.B \-V, \-\-version
Show the program version and the SQLite engine version.
.SH ITEM IDS
\fBlist\fR shows the last eight hex characters of each item id. \fBrestore\fR,
\fBempty\fR, and \fBwhere\fR accept that short id, any unique prefix of it, a
unique prefix of the full id, or the full id printed by \fB\-\-long\fR or
\fBlist --ids\fR.
.SH SIZES
\fBput\fR only moves the payload; the size of an item is measured the first
time \fBlist\fR shows it and is then stored in the item metadata. The size is
the apparent size of a file, or the sum of the apparent sizes of the files
inside a directory; symlinks are never followed and special files contribute
nothing. Items whose size is not known yet show \fB-\fR, and the total below
the table sums only the known sizes and reports how many items are unknown.
Use \fBtrash doctor --repair\fR to measure every unknown item at once.
.SH SECURITY
\fBtrash\fR keeps its store private: it must be owned by the current user and
must not be writable by the group or others (mode 0700). \fBitems/\fR,
\fB.gc/\fR and the lock file must be real files and
directories, never symlinks; a symlinked item is reported as broken and is not
repaired, restored, or purged through. \fBput\fR and \fBrestore\fR walk the
parent directory one component at a time without following symlinks and move
the payload with a rename relative to the opened directory, so a component
swapped for a symlink between validation and the move cannot redirect it.
\fBrestore\fR also refuses to write when the parent of the original path no
longer resolves to itself (for example, a component was replaced with a
symlink after the item was trashed).
\fB\-\-reset-store\fR wipes the store when its index schema is
incompatible; without it an interactive run asks for confirmation and a
non-interactive run leaves the store untouched. Wiping permanently deletes
every trashed item. Trashing is not secure erasure: payload bytes may remain on
disk.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B index.db
SQLite index and the source of truth. It contains original paths, sizes and
ids, so treat it as sensitive metadata.
.TP
.B index.db-wal, index.db-shm
Write-ahead log and shared-memory file, present while the store is in use.
They belong to the live database, not to a backup.
.TP
.B index.backup.db
Consistent snapshot written by \fBdoctor --backup\fR.
.TP
.B items/
One directory per item; \fBitems/<id>/data\fR is the payload.
.TP
.B .gc/
Items being permanently deleted.
.TP
.B .lock
Advisory lock for destructive operations.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B TRASH_STORE
Default trash store.
.TP
.B XDG_DATA_HOME
Used to derive \fB$XDG_DATA_HOME/trash\fR.
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
Disable colours when set.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success, 1 on operational failure, 2 on invalid arguments.
.SH EXAMPLES
trash report.txt
.PP
trash -r build/
.PP
trash list -r
.PP
trash list --all --grep report --ids | trash where
.PP
trash where 8bd42d97
.PP
trash stats
.PP
trash restore -i
.PP
trash restore
.PP
trash restore --all --limit 1
.PP
trash empty --all --yes
.PP
trash empty --older-than 30d --keep 5 --yes
.PP
trash empty --all --dry-run
.PP
trash doctor --repair
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR rm (1)
